{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "polarity",
      "components": ["Q12", "U42"],
      "pins": ["Q12.1", "Q12.2", "Q12.3", "U42.1", "U42.5", "U42.6"],
      "nets": ["NET0102", "+5V", "Net-(Q12-G)"],
      "observed": "Q12’s gate is driven by LTC4412 U42, but its source is connected to +5V and its drain to USB VBUS. U42 monitors USB VBUS at VIN and +5V at SENSE.",
      "expected": "For the intended LTC4412 ideal-diode path from USB VBUS to +5V, Q12 should have its source on the input side (USB VBUS) and drain on the output side (+5V).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"+5V\") ... (node (ref \"Q12\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"S\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"NET0102\") ... (node (ref \"Q12\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"D\")) ... (node (ref \"U42\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"VIN\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"Net-(Q12-G)\") (node (ref \"Q12\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"G\")) (node (ref \"U42\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GATE\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"+5V\") ... (node (ref \"U42\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"SENSE\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "`U6` — LTC4412xS6 ... USB power-path controller intended to OR/protect a USB power input"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The reversed MOSFET orientation undermines the intended ideal-diode control: the body-diode path and controller’s gate action are not arranged for the specified input-to-output power path, risking failure to power the +5V rail correctly from USB or inadequate reverse-current blocking.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Anomaly detected: Q12 is oriented opposite to the input-to-output MOSFET arrangement required for the LTC4412 USB ideal-diode path. This can impair USB power delivery and reverse-current protection."
}

